This single-producer/single-consumer lock-free ring buffer passes tests on x86 but occasionally returns garbage values on ARM. Identify all memory-ordering and correctness bugs, explain why x86 masks them, and provide a corrected implementation with the weakest correct memory orders, justifying each one. Also comment on false sharing.

```cpp
template <typename T, size_t N>
class SpscQueue {
    T buf[N];
    std::atomic<size_t> head{0}; // consumer reads here
    std::atomic<size_t> tail{0}; // producer writes here
public:
    bool push(const T& v) {
        size_t t = tail.load(std::memory_order_relaxed);
        size_t next = (t + 1) % N;
        if (next == head.load(std::memory_order_relaxed)) return false;
        tail.store(next, std::memory_order_relaxed);
        buf[t] = v;
        return true;
    }
    bool pop(T& out) {
        size_t h = head.load(std::memory_order_relaxed);
        if (h == tail.load(std::memory_order_relaxed)) return false;
        out = buf[h];
        head.store((h + 1) % N, std::memory_order_relaxed);
        return true;
    }
};
```

